Article 5 Foreign-invested projects falling under any of the following circumstances shall be classified as encouraged:
(1) belonging to new agricultural technologies, comprehensive agricultural development, and energy, transportation, and important raw material industries;
(2) belonging to high and new technologies or advanced applicable technologies, capable of improving product performance, enhancing the technological and economic benefits of enterprises, or producing new equipment or new materials that are in short supply domestically;
(3) meeting market demand, capable of upgrading product grades, opening up emerging markets, or increasing the international competitiveness of products;
(4) belonging to new technologies or new equipment, capable of saving energy and raw materials, comprehensively utilizing resources and renewable resources, and preventing environmental pollution;
(5) capable of leveraging the human resources and resource advantages of the central and western regions and conforming to the state's industrial policies; or
(6) other circumstances as provided by laws or administrative regulations.

Article 6 Foreign-invested projects falling under any of the following circumstances shall be classified as restricted:
(1) having backward technological levels;
(2) being detrimental to resource conservation or the improvement of the ecological environment;
(3) engaging in the exploration and exploitation of specific minerals subject to protective exploitation as prescribed by the state;
(4) belonging to industries that the state gradually opens up; or
(5) other circumstances as provided by laws or administrative regulations.

Article 7 Foreign-invested projects falling under any of the following circumstances shall be classified as prohibited:
(1) endangering national security or harming the public interest;
(2) causing pollution damage to the environment, destroying natural resources, or harming human health;
(3) occupying large amounts of arable land and being detrimental to the protection and development of land resources;
(4) endangering the safety or operational effectiveness of military facilities;
(5) using unique Chinese processes or technologies to produce products; or
(6) other circumstances as provided by laws or administrative regulations.
